{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner, amending spike three d719428f: the tiny inventory UI is built in the inventory repository, not under spike/. Everything else in that request stands, including the gating.\n\nWHY. Hugh asked today for a concrete inventory application in its own repo, and chose to bootstrap it now in parallel rather than wait. I have filed that as 4040a6f2, addressed to codex: own repository at /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-inventory, own genesis and durable log, origin https://github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq-inventory.git. Once that exists, building spike three's UI under gitseq's spike/ tree would put the inventory application's user interface in a different repository from the inventory application. That is the wrong place for it and it would have to move later.\n\nWHAT CHANGES IN d719428f. The UI half of its scope moves to the inventory repository: it is built there, its artifacts are filed in that workroom, and its review happens there. The JSONata reference compatibility and determinism corpus does not move. That corpus is about the platform's JSONata semantics rather than about inventory, it belongs beside spike/jsonataddl where the other two spikes live, and splitting it out would strand it.\n\nWHAT DOES NOT CHANGE. The gating stands exactly as written: do not promise d719428f until spike one (396dc130, claude) and spike two (466a084e, codex, landed at 7e88b9aa) have both reported, and state in the promise what each concluded. The four demonstration steps stand — discovers its schema, submits an event, waits for the frontier, re-runs a SQL query — as does the requirement to say what the frontier wait actually observes and what happens if the frontier never advances. The mutation-sensitivity conditions at the frontier-wait and corpus-comparison sites stand. The kernel boundary stands.\n\nONE CONSEQUENCE WORTH NAMING RATHER THAN DISCOVERING LATER. d719428f will then deliver across two repositories, which means two heads, two artifact sets and two reviews, not one. If that makes the request the wrong shape, say so and I will split it into a corpus request here and a UI request in the inventory workroom rather than have you reinterpret it. I would rather refile than have you guess at what I meant.\n\nThis amendment is filed as an assert rather than a refile deliberately: d719428f is unpromised and gated, so there is no in-flight work to cancel, and retiring it would only cost a row and a re-read. A reviewer of that lane should read this assert as part of its conditions."}